#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1afe82-86ba-5a15-9037-74717692ecb2" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To a Coefficients Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "836f2f1b-5dd4-5dba-81cc-eb9ffe7db8d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4693" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2:= R_2 +(-4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A$?" :question/worked-solution "The operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1\n$$\n\nmeans that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row.\n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-4) \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 + (-4)(1) & 2 + (-4)(2) & -1 + (-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c27a3f3c-77c7-55e9-8714-1f24c4a48063" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To an Augmented Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2ff0a6f5-f3b2-5b39-8b69-1a0c969548cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-4694" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A$?" :question/worked-solution "The operation\n\n$$\nR_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2\n$$\n\nmeans that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}R_{1}↔R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "691b9107-a935-5965-b3ac-0f3a193ce9bd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform One Matrix Into Another"}] :question/id #uuid "a24c9621-793b-5c60-9c47-2b452f0faa2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4695" :question/problem "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row.\n\nNow, applying the operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 1 \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 + 1 \\cdot (-4) & 1 + 1 \\cdot (-2) & 2 + 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059507 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0464b51e-8568-521e-96a5-a65c04569c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform a Given Entry to a Desired Value"}] :question/id #uuid "f8417bbc-b748-519b-a997-fbb4aed755eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4696" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0$?" :question/worked-solution "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0$.\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0$, because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0$.\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1$. If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22}$, we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-5) \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 + (-5) \\cdot 3 & 5 + (-5) \\cdot 1 & 6 + (-5) \\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 -5 R_1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c27a3f3c-77c7-55e9-8714-1f24c4a48063" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To an Augmented Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92e38ca4-c738-5e47-a590-b209a3d19694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056007} {:answer/id #uuid "eda762ac-ee3e-50f6-b681-57fa7be2c3f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056008} {:answer/id #uuid "093a0c72-f1b7-5143-b70c-d653458bfae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 1\\end{bma